# Practical 2 – Linear Search and Binary Search

## Aim
To implement Linear Search and Binary Search in Python and compare their time complexities.

## Linear Search
Checks each element one by one until the target is found.

**Time Complexity:** O(n)

## Binary Search
Searches a sorted list by repeatedly dividing it into two halves.

**Time Complexity:** O(log n)

## Sample Input
```text
Enter numbers separated by spaces: 10 20 30 40 50 60
Enter number to search: 40
````

## Output

```text
Linear Search:
40 found at index 3
Time Complexity: O(n)

Binary Search:
40 found at index 3
Time Complexity: O(log n)
```

## Comparison

| Feature              | Linear Search | Binary Search |
| -------------------- | ------------- | ------------- |
| Sorted list required | No            | Yes           |
| Best Case            | O(1)          | O(1)          |
| Average Case         | O(n)          | O(log n)      |
| Worst Case           | O(n)          | O(log n)      |

## Conclusion

Linear Search is simple and works on unsorted data, while Binary Search is faster but requires sorted data.

